window.docContents[204].resolve({"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L3-___-The-body-opens-up/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Synopsis--Purpose":{"contents":"Open body A of pair P into two rigid halves, with a body-fixed frame beside the lab frame. Mass assembles over the halves as a sum. The centre of mass is the mass-weighted mean of the halves' positions, which is meaningful only when the total mass is nonzero. Every half of a rigid body has the same angular velocity, so angular velocity is intensive and has no Assembles instance. Each frame-dependent quantity has its frame in its type (InFrame), so a body-frame rotational energy computed from a lab-frame angular velocity fails to type-check.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L3 — The body opens up\u0009Synopsis","header":"5.4.1.1. Purpose","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L3-___-The-body-opens-up/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Synopsis--Purpose"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L4-___-Out-of-the-line/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L4-___-Out-of-the-line--Lesson":{"contents":"1. Points, vectors, and distances. PairState3 π stores this state's centre-of-mass\npoints. BodyOf π keeps a body's nominal pair identity, while SeparationOf π names\nthe pair's A–B separation. The upstream positionQ, displacementQ, and distanceQ\nare applied to the state fields. Position plus displacement and distance plus the\nlength of that displacement are both pinned refusals:\n\n\n\n\n\n2. Whose distance. distanceQ returns a Quantity and so erases the HCP pair\nbearer. separationOf puts that same distance on SeparationOf π. Distance is a\nratio-scale length species, so DifferenceKind.ofScale applies: two records under P\ncan express a change in separation, while Q's pair bearer does not unify with P's.\n\n\n\n3. Distance is displacement length. These laws are applied to the state’s own A and B\ncentres. The magnitudes agree, but the kinds remain distinct; the triangle law is another\nstatement over the points carried by one PairState3.\n\n\n\n4. Bare frames. The vector fed to the three upstream laws is derived from this state's\ntwo centres. All three transports accept it because F.Vector records a frame but not a\nphysical role. The displacement-plus-origin-velocity sum also elaborates. Both are\naccepted but physically wrong when that vector is meant to be a displacement.\n\n\n\n5. Kinded frames. Frame components must enter through the frame's own dispEquiv.\nWith KVector, the displacement goes through geometric transport, and an average\nvelocity computed from two same-pair centre states goes through the boost law. The\nobserver-independence theorem shows that geometric transport preserves the displacement.\nThe wrong boost on a displacement and a velocity-plus-force sum are pinned refusals.\n\n\n\n6. The observer moves. origin_displacement_eq states the cart frame's own origin\nmotion over elapsed time. The pair's state-derived displacement still cannot be added to\nthat frame's kinded velocity.\n\n\n\n7. Adoption ladder. The five Space stages go from Kinds, through Metrology,\nKinded and Operators, to Audits. Stage 3's operator-built area is computed from this\nstate's A–B displacement and a probe-point displacement; its magnitude law is accepted,\nwhile area × area is refused. Stage 4 audits the boundary and anchors the API map.\n\n\n\nThe Space adoption files, in order, are ForPhysLib/Kinds/Space.lean,\nForPhysLib/Metrology/Space.lean, ForPhysLib/Kinded/Space.lean,\nForPhysLib/Operators/Space.lean, and ForPhysLib/Audits/Space.lean. The final audit\nstage is wired to Space/API-map.md by Space.checked_by.yaml.\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L4 — Out of the line","header":"5.5.7. Lesson","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L4-___-Out-of-the-line/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L4-___-Out-of-the-line--Lesson"}});